\begin{abstract}
  Majority voting counts assertions, reliability weighting scales them by agent accuracy, and recent work derives optimal weights from higher-order agreement structure. Every method in that line reads one object: which agents asserted a claim. That discards most of what open-ended generation reveals. An agent does not vote, it produces a structured output, and where a claim sits in that output, and how much the agent produced before omitting it, are observations about it. Write $S(c)$ for the asserting agents and $B(c)$ for what their generations reveal: majority voting reads $|S(c)|$, reliability weighting reads $S(c)$, we read both. Any $S(c)$ rule is available to a rule using $(S(c), B(c))$, so the second cannot be worse in the population; whether it helps is empirical. On QAMPARI it does, and the identity channel is near exhausted: the best rule estimable from $S(c)$ alone beats counting by $+0.30$ F1, interval $[-0.34, +0.90]$, while generated position and graded omission reach $30.68$ and a metric-matched keep-count reaches $31.70$ against $27.40$ for the best single agent. The gain holds in all 210 agent subsets of size three to six and keeps $92\%$ of its value when training folds exclude the held-out question type. On ASQA the behavioural channel is empty, our scorer falls below the identity-only rule it builds on, and reliability weighting wins. One statistic from the generations separates the two regimes.
  % [claim:C001] [evidence:E100]
  % [claim:C002] [evidence:E100,E101]
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

When several language models answer the same question, the combination step is usually a vote over whole responses \cite{li2024agents} or a learned selection or fusion of them \cite{jiang2023llm}, and the sophistication has gone into what each vote is worth: majority voting treats agents equally, reliability weighting scales them by accuracy, recent work derives optimal weights from higher-order agreement structure \cite{ai2025majority}. Every method in that line reads one object: for a given claim, which agents asserted it. That fits an agent choosing one of a fixed set of labels, and fits open-ended generation poorly, where an agent produces a structured artifact --- an ordered list, or a paragraph of separable statements. Figure~\ref{fig:hierarchy} shows two claims from our pool with exactly this problem. The same agent asserted each and no other agent did, so every rule in that line reads them identically; but one sits first in a three-item list, and the other twenty-fifth in a list of 101, beside an agent that produced 2,398 items without ever mentioning it. The first is correct and the second is not.

Getting this wrong is easy and expensive. On our entity-list pool a strict majority vote scores $22.80$ set F1 against the best single agent's $27.92$: eight times the budget, less accuracy. % [claim:C003] [evidence:E121]
Response-level selection fails differently: no two open-ended responses coincide, so a global-weight rule always returns the highest-weighted agent, reproducing best-single exactly at $+0.00$ with a zero-width interval. % [claim:C004] [evidence:E121]
The published weighting theory does not transfer either: it targets binary and categorical predictions, and on set-valued answers its log-odds weight $\log(p/(1-p))$ needs per-agent accuracy above $0.5$ while per-claim precision here runs $5.8\%$ to $28\%$, so every weight is negative and the optimal threshold switches the filter off. % [claim:C005] [evidence:E002,E121]
The nearest published system, atomic self-consistency \cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic}, handles set-valued answers directly but draws its $m$ samples from one model, so agent identity is not a variable; claim-level cross-model filters \cite{goel2025zero,ning2025mad,wan2025fastfact} score claims for factuality but never use a claim's placement in the generating model's own output, grade silence, or choose how many claims to return. No principled account exists of how to weight heterogeneous agents when answers are sets, or of what such weighting can and cannot buy.

For a candidate claim $c$, write $S(c)$ for the agents that asserted it and $B(c)$ for what their generations reveal about it: the position each asserting agent gave it, and how much each silent agent produced instead. Majority voting reads the count $|S(c)|$; reliability weighting, correlation-aware weighting and annotator-reliability models \cite{dawid1979maximum} read the identity set $S(c)$; we read both. The identity channel then has a well-defined best case: any rule seeing only $S(c)$ partitions candidates into cells and decides once per cell, so estimating $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c))$ and thresholding it is the strongest such rule we can build, converting an open design space into one number. And since any $S$-only rule remains available to a rule that also sees $B$, the richer observation cannot hurt in the population; whether it helps is empirical. We ask what is left in the identity channel once agreement count has been used (RQ1), whether generation behaviour carries more (RQ2), and whether we can tell in advance, from the generations alone, which channel has the headroom (RQ3). The answer to the last differs sharply by task, and is predictable.


\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig0_hierarchy.pdf}
  \caption{Two QAMPARI claims that no published aggregation rule can separate. Each was asserted by \texttt{Kimi-K2.5} and by no other agent, so the count $|S(c)|$ and the identity set $S(c)$ are identical for both, and every rule of Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} must give them the same decision. They differ only in $B(c)$: the correct claim is the first of three items that agent produced, while the incorrect one is the twenty-fifth of 101, and the agents that stayed silent on it had produced 2,398 and 791 items of their own. Columns are the eight agents in a fixed order, most verbose first.}
  \label{fig:hierarchy}
\end{figure}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{An information decomposition of multi-agent aggregation} into the count $|S(c)|$, the identity set $S(c)$ and generation behaviour $B(c)$, with the observation that the identity channel can saturate: on QAMPARI the strongest identity-only rule we can estimate beats counting by $+0.30$ set F1, interval $[-0.34, +0.90]$.
% [claim:C006] [evidence:E100]
\item \textbf{An instantiation of the behavioural channel} for list-structured output, using a claim's position in each asserting agent's output and the amount each silent agent produced instead, plus a metric-matched decision rule. It reaches $31.70$ F1, $+4.30$ over the best single agent and $+2.50$ over counting, both intervals excluding zero.
% [claim:C007] [evidence:E100]
\item \textbf{Evidence that the behavioural channel transfers better:} the gain holds in all 210 agent subsets of size three to six and survives holding out a whole question type, while the identity-only rule is indistinguishable from counting under either protocol.
\item \textbf{A diagnostic for which channel has headroom}, computable from the generations, and the benchmark where it correctly predicts our own instantiation will fail.
\end{enumerate}

$B(c)$ is a slot rather than a fixed feature list, and our identity-channel claim is empirical rather than a theorem; Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} states its limits.


\section{Background}
\label{sec:background}

\paragraph{Task.}
Each of $n$ agents $M = \{m_1, \dots, m_n\}$ answers question $q$ once, producing an ordered sequence of claims. Claims are normalised and pooled into a candidate set $C(q)$; candidate $c$ is \emph{asserted} by $m$ if $m$ produced a claim normalising to $c$. The aggregator outputs a subset of $C(q)$, scored against gold $G(q)$.

\paragraph{Support pattern.}
$S(c) \subseteq M$ is the set of agents asserting $c$, with cardinality $|S(c)|$ the \emph{support count}. It also determines who stayed silent, $M \setminus S(c)$.

\paragraph{Generation behaviour.}
$B(c)$ collects what the generations reveal about $c$ beyond assertion: for an asserting agent, where in its output the claim appeared; for a silent agent, how much it produced instead. Appendix~\ref{sec:theory} states what separating $S(c)$ from $B(c)$ buys; Section~\ref{sec:slotA} gives our list-structured instantiation.

\paragraph{Within-output position.}
$r_m(c)$ is the 1-indexed position of $c$ in agent $m$'s own output for $q$, defined only for $m \in S(c)$; $L_m(q)$ is how many claims $m$ produced for $q$. Both are properties of the generating agent's output, not of any list supplied to it.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
On QAMPARI we report set F1 against $G(q)$, macro-averaged over questions. On ASQA we report $\mathrm{DR}^* = \sqrt{\mathrm{ROUGE\text-L} \times \mathrm{STR\text-EM}}$, where ROUGE-L is an F-measure over the longest common subsequence with the reference answer and STR-EM is the fraction of gold short answers appearing verbatim in the generated text. This is a proxy for the published Disambig-ROUGE (DR), the geometric mean of ROUGE-L and Disambig-F1, the latter scoring short answers recovered from the generated text by a RoBERTa-SQuADv2 reader rather than matched literally. The ASQA authors state that STR-EM and Disambig-F1 measure the same aspect, which licenses the substitution \cite{stelmakh2022asqa}; the proxy avoids standing up that reader and is labelled as a proxy throughout.
% [claim:C008] [evidence:E116,E012]
On TriviaQA we report accuracy.


\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{The support pattern and its ceiling}
\label{sec:ceiling}

Consider any rule whose keep-or-drop decision for $c$ depends on $c$ only through $S(c)$: weighted voting with per-agent weights; Naive Bayes over per-agent true- and false-positive rates (TPR, FPR), with or without silence as evidence; any correlation- or diversity-aware weighting, since the pairwise co-assertion structure it reads is itself a function of $S(c)$; a Dawid-Skene annotator model \cite{dawid1979maximum}.

Seeing only $S(c)$, the rule partitions candidates into at most $2^n$ cells and decides once per cell. For any objective monotone in the probability a kept claim is correct, the best per-cell decision follows from $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c) = s)$; estimating that on training folds and thresholding it is the strongest rule of this form we can construct, and gives one number for what the identity channel is worth on a dataset. Counting is a member of the class, restricting attention to $|S(c)|$. Figure~\ref{fig:ceiling} measures what separates the two on QAMPARI: the headroom a rule reading $S(c)$ has over one reading $|S(c)|$ is widest exactly where the fewest claims are.

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig2_ceiling.pdf}
  \caption{What the identity channel has to work with on QAMPARI. Upper: how many of the 110,136 pooled claims sit at each support count. Lower: the headroom at that support count, the spread in correctness across the support patterns holding at least 30 claims, which is what a rule reading $S(c)$ can exploit over one reading $|S(c)|$ alone. The two panels have opposite shapes: $87\%$ of all claims sit at $|S(c)|=1$, where the headroom is $5.5$ points, and the widest headroom of $59$ points sits at $|S(c)|=4$, which holds $1{,}655$ claims.}
  \label{fig:ceiling}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Limits of this construction.}
The lookup is a finite-sample shrunk estimate of $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c))$, so it is an empirical benchmark rather than an attained bound. And set F1 is not decomposable: a rule deciding jointly across a question's candidates could in principle beat any per-candidate threshold rule, the form every arm here takes. The claim is therefore that the lookup is the strongest \emph{claim-local, identity-only} rule we can estimate, not that no use of agent identity can do better --- enough to bound the design space we would otherwise search by trial and error.


The construction bounds the \emph{population} optimum. With finite data the lookup must estimate up to $2^n$ cells, so a constrained rule can beat it in sample; we observe this on ASQA (Section~\ref{sec:rq3}) and read it as a statement about data volume, not a counterexample.
% [claim:C009] [evidence:E103]


\subsection{A template with two typed slots}


Stages 1, 2 and 4 of the pipeline are identical across every benchmark; stage 1 varies mechanically with how answers are delimited --- normalised entity strings for lists, sentence clusters for prose, terse answers for short-answer QA. Stages 3 and 5 are the genuine slots, and Table~\ref{tab:slots} states what fixes each.

\begin{table}
  \caption{The two slots and what fixes them. Stage 3 is fixed by the answer format: position inside a list exists only if the agent emitted a list. Stage 5 is fixed by the metric: set F1 admits a keep-count, a recall-only metric does not, a single-answer metric admits only an argmax. Both are fixed before any benchmark is run.}
  \label{tab:slots}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \toprule
    Stage & QAMPARI & ASQA & TriviaQA \\
    \midrule
    1 pool to units       & entity strings   & sentence clusters  & terse answers      \\
    2 support pattern     & identical        & identical          & identical          \\
    \textbf{3 confidence signal} & list rank, omission & sentence position & self-consistency \\
    4 cross-fitted logistic & identical      & identical          & identical          \\
    \textbf{5 decision rule}     & per-question budget & swept threshold & argmax     \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

The template carries a precondition, made operational in Section~\ref{sec:screen}: stage 3 is worth filling only where the Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} ceiling binds.

\subsection{The confidence slot for list answers}
\label{sec:slotA}

For list answers we fill stage 3 with three feature groups, all computed from the agents' own outputs and none a function of $S(c)$ alone. Exact definitions are in Appendix~\ref{app:features}; the construction is a single function in the released code.
% [claim:C010] [evidence:E113]

\paragraph{Within-output position.}
For each asserting agent, $\log(1 + r_m(c))$ and relative position $r_m(c)/L_m(q)$, summarised across asserters by min, mean, max and standard deviation, plus one per-agent term so the model can learn that position means different things for different agents.

\paragraph{Per-agent position bands.}
A one-hot encoding of $r_m(c)$ into seven bands per agent, letting the scorer express non-monotone dependence on position without a non-linear model.

\paragraph{Omission strength.}
Every prior rule we know of treats silence as one thing: Naive Bayes assigns every silent agent the same $\log((1-\mathrm{TPR})/(1-\mathrm{FPR}))$, and a logistic over support indicators assigns zero. List lengths here span a median of 1 for the smallest agent to a mean of $72.2$ for the largest.
% [claim:C011] [evidence:E107]
An agent that emitted three items without mentioning $c$ has said little about $q$; one that emitted fifty has said a great deal about $q$ without saying $c$. We therefore weight each silent agent by $\log L_m(q)$ and enter the sum, the maximum, the count with $L_m(q) \geq 20$, and one per-agent term. We know of no prior work that grades silence this way.

\paragraph{Stacked pattern probability.}
The training-fold estimate of $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c) = s)$ as a single logit-scaled feature, so the scorer takes the ceiling arm's output as input rather than competing with it.

\paragraph{Scorer.}
An L2-regularised logistic regression with ridge coefficient $1.0$ over 92 features.
% [claim:C012] [evidence:E113,E114]
Asserter verbosity is excluded: it adds $+0.01$ over position, and the controlled analysis is in Appendix~\ref{app:features}. List length survives only inside omission strength, where a silent agent has no position and length is the only evidence.

\subsection{The decision slot for set F1}
\label{sec:slotB}

Scoring produces an ordering; something must decide how far down to cut. QAMPARI gold sets range from 5 to 40 with a mean of $10.2$, so one global threshold is wrong on most questions.
% [claim:C013] [evidence:E112]

For an F1 over sets, take candidates in descending predicted probability $p_1 \geq p_2 \geq \cdots$, substitute $\sum_{i \leq k} p_i$ for the unknown correct count among the top $k$, and $\hat{G}_q$ for the unknown gold size:
\[
  k^*(q) \;=\; \arg\max_k \; \frac{2 \sum_{i \leq k} p_i}{k + \hat{G}_q}.
\]
$\hat{G}_q$ is a ridge regression fitted on training questions only, over quantities available at inference: candidate count, maximum support count, how many candidates have at least two supporters, and the sum and maximum of predicted probabilities.

The estimate need not be accurate: its mean absolute error is $4.62$ against a gold mean of $10.2$, substituting the true count gains only $+0.55$, and a single constant $k$ across all questions gains nothing over counting, $29.41$ against $29.21$.
% [claim:C014] [evidence:E112,E100]


Appendix~\ref{app:budget} works the curve through on two held-out questions, one where the argmax lands on the true optimum and one where it cuts short, $\hat{G}_q$ wrong in both. The gain comes from the cut varying per question, not from its accuracy.


\subsection{Cross-fitting}

Every fitted object --- pattern lookup, logistic coefficients, gold-count ridge, per-agent weights, every threshold --- is fitted on training folds and applied to held-out questions. We use 5 folds over questions on QAMPARI and 2 on ASQA, where each threshold evaluation costs a ROUGE-L pass. Intervals are paired bootstrap over questions with 10,000 resamples.

Cross-fitting is not a formality on this data: selecting a keep threshold on the evaluation set rather than on training folds moved one measured gain from $+0.08$ to $-1.25$, so we treat any in-sample tuned number as uninformative. % [claim:C015] [evidence:E111]

\subsection{Screening a dataset before filling the slot}
\label{sec:screen}

Filling the confidence slot is expensive: it means deciding what signal the format affords, implementing it, and labelling enough claims to fit a scorer. Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} says it is worth filling only where the ceiling binds, and this statistic estimates that from the agents' outputs:
\[
  \text{solo ratio} \;=\; \frac{P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \text{exactly 1 agent asserts})}{P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \geq 2 \text{ agents assert})}.
\]
A low ratio means a \emph{solo} claim, one asserted by exactly one agent, is strong evidence of being wrong, so a support-count threshold already filters well and weighting has little to add; the remaining signal must come from elsewhere. A high ratio means counting discards most of the content for little precision gain, so only weighting can filter. The share of solo candidates carries none of this: it is roughly $87\%$ on both QAMPARI and ASQA.
% [claim:C016] [evidence:E118]


\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Benchmarks}

\paragraph{Why these benchmarks.}
The behavioural channel is a filter, and a filter trades precision against recall, so a benchmark can adjudicate one only if its metric scores both. That constrains measurement rather than the method, and is why the benchmark where we measure the full pipeline is an entity-list task. The three below differ in answer format so that the two slots of Section~\ref{sec:method} must be filled differently in each.

\paragraph{QAMPARI \cite{amouyal2022qampari}.}
777 questions with entity-list answers, scored by set F1. Each list item is natively an atomic fact, so no sentence splitting or summarisation is required, and the aggregator's output cannot contain content absent from some agent's response. Grading is programmatic against gold aliases, no judge model. But $97.2\%$ of gold entries ship no alias beyond the answer text, so grading is close to exact string match and correct answers phrased differently score zero; a strict-versus-relaxed sensitivity analysis puts this at roughly $-7$ F1 across every arm, changing no conclusion because the penalty is near-uniform.
% [claim:C017] [evidence:E119]

Questions come from five structurally different sources (\texttt{wikidata\_simple}, \texttt{wikidata\_intersection}, \texttt{wikidata\_comp}, \texttt{wikitables\_composition}, \texttt{wikitables\_simple}), which we use for the distribution-shift protocol of Section~\ref{sec:robust}.
% [claim:C018] [evidence:E101]

\paragraph{ASQA \cite{stelmakh2022asqa}.}
400 ambiguous questions with long-form answers, decomposed into sentence clusters, scored by the $\mathrm{DR}^*$ proxy of Section~\ref{sec:background}.

\paragraph{TriviaQA.}
2,757 short-answer questions, scored by accuracy. Each response is a single answer, so within-output position and the keep-count are both undefined. TriviaQA enters as a third point for the screen of Section~\ref{sec:screen} and as the benchmark where self-consistency fills the confidence slot.

\subsection{Agent pool}

Eight agents on QAMPARI: \texttt{grok-4.3}, \texttt{Kimi-K2.5}, \texttt{MAI-Thinking-1}, \texttt{gpt-5.4}, \texttt{DeepSeek-V4-Flash}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-mini}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano}, and \texttt{Cohere-command-a-plus-05-2026}. Six on ASQA: \texttt{DeepSeek-V4-Flash}, \texttt{MAI-Thinking-1}, \texttt{gpt-5.4}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-mini}, \texttt{gpt-5.4-nano}, and \texttt{grok-4.3}. % [claim:C019] [evidence:E115,E104]
Six on TriviaQA. Each agent is sampled once per question at a single decoding setting, and every result here is computed from that one set of generations. Appendix~\ref{app:agents} reports the per-agent list-length spread omission strength depends on: agents differ by more than an order of magnitude in how much they emit.


% [claim:C020] [evidence:E107]

\subsection{Baselines}

\emph{Best single agent} is the pool member with the highest mean score, selected under the same cross-fitting protocol as every other arm. \emph{Multi-agent count} keeps $c$ if $|S(c)| \geq \theta$; \emph{reliability weighting} keeps $c$ if $\sum_{m \in S(c)} w_m \geq \Theta$; the \emph{pattern lookup} keeps $c$ if the training-fold estimate of $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid S(c))$ exceeds a threshold, and is the ceiling of Section~\ref{sec:ceiling}, in substance a Dawid-Skene annotator model \cite{dawid1979maximum} applied to language-model agents, and a baseline here rather than a contribution. All thresholds and weights are fitted on training folds, and counting is our own naive baseline rather than a published method. We also run the rest of the weighting family --- marginal per-agent precision, solo precision, Naive Bayes with and without silence as evidence, correlation-aware weighting --- and the classical rank-fusion rules: Reciprocal Rank Fusion \cite{cormack2009reciprocal}; Borda, which scores each item by its rank position in every list; and CombSUM and CombMNZ, which sum normalised scores across lists, the latter weighting by how many lists contain the item.

For ASC we run the published list pipeline. We verified against the paper that its list configuration has no LLM composition step and that its final answer is the selected cluster representatives, so our no-composition configuration is the paper's pipeline rather than a truncation of it.
% [claim:C021] [evidence:E120,E001]
One substitution remains: ASC draws $m$ stochastic samples from one model, while we have one response per agent, so agents stand in for samples. That changes the estimand from self-consistency to inter-model agreement, and the single-model form is not evaluated here.

\subsection{Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

5-fold cross-fitting over questions on QAMPARI, and 2-fold on ASQA. Paired bootstrap over questions with 10,000 resamples and $95\%$ intervals; $*$ marks an interval excluding zero. The per-ensemble sweeps evaluate all 210 subsets of sizes 3 to 6 on QAMPARI and all 42 subsets of sizes 3 to 6 on ASQA.

\paragraph{One data-hygiene note.}
Generations from one agent contained literal special-token strings that survived into the parsed item lists, which normalisation mapped to 1,244 of 110,801 unmatched candidate strings, none matching gold. This padded every arm's precision denominator and, more importantly, split candidates that should have merged: a claim emitted with a leaked prefix by one agent and without one by another counted as two support-1 candidates rather than one with support 2. Stripping the tokens leaves 110,136 candidates, changes every arm by at most $0.14$ F1, alters no significance verdict, and grows our margin over counting from $+2.39$ to $+2.50$. All QAMPARI numbers in Section~\ref{sec:results} are post-fix unless marked otherwise.
% [claim:C022] [evidence:E117,E100]


\section{Results and analysis}
\label{sec:results}

After pooling and normalisation the QAMPARI pool is 110,136 candidates across 777 questions, $3.57\%$ correct and 95,772 ($87.0\%$) asserted by exactly one agent.
% [claim:C023] [evidence:E100] [evidence:E124]

Figure~\ref{fig:worked} works one held-out question end to end, with every quantity of Section~\ref{sec:background} instantiated on real data.


\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_worked_example.pdf}
  \caption{One held-out question end to end. Rows are the pooled candidates $C(q)$; filled cells are the support pattern $S(c)$, the number inside the within-output position $r_m(c)$; column headers give each agent's output length $L_m(q)$; blank cells are the omissions that omission strength grades by $\log L_m(q)$. Support alone would not separate these rows: \texttt{barchana} has four asserters and \texttt{jajpur road} one, yet the ordering keeps all seven gold answers and drops all seven non-answers. A dot marks an agent that did not assert the candidate; \texttt{Cohere} returned nothing here, so $L_m=0$ and the omission feature clamps $\log L_m$ at $L_m=1$. The keep-count $k^*(q)=7$ is computed from $p$ and the predicted gold size $\hat{G}_q=9.38$ alone (Section~\ref{sec:slotB}); the true count is 7. \texttt{barchana} and \texttt{barachana} differ by one character and are graded as different entities, the effect discussed in Section~\ref{sec:negative}.}
  \label{fig:worked}
\end{figure}

\subsection{RQ1: what reliability weighting is worth, and its bound}
\label{sec:rq1}

\begin{table}
  \caption{QAMPARI main results: 777 questions, 8 agents, 5-fold cross-fitted by question, paired bootstrap with 10,000 resamples. The best rule expressible from the support pattern alone, which upper-bounds every reliability-weighting scheme, beats counting by $+0.30$ F1, interval $[-0.34, +0.90]$. Everything above about $+0.9$ comes from information the support pattern does not contain.}
  \label{tab:main}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrll}
    \toprule
    Arm & Set F1 & vs.\ best single & vs.\ count \\
    \midrule
    best single agent (\texttt{Kimi-K2.5})  & 27.40 & (reference)              & $-1.80$ \\
    multi-agent count, $\theta$ cross-fitted & 29.21 & $+1.80\ [+0.76,+2.87]^*$ & (reference) \\
    pattern lookup (ceiling on all weighting) & 29.50 & $+2.10\ [+1.09,+3.09]^*$ & $+0.30\ [-0.34,+0.90]$ \\
    ours: scorer only, global threshold     & 30.68 & $+3.28\ [+2.31,+4.26]^*$ & $+1.47\ [+0.78,+2.14]^*$ \\
    \textbf{ours: scorer + budget}          & \textbf{31.70} & $\mathbf{+4.30\ [+3.37,+5.26]^*}$ & $\mathbf{+2.50\ [+1.72,+3.28]^*}$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C024] [evidence:E100]

Table~\ref{tab:main} answers RQ1 with a bound: at $95\%$ the data place any rule in the class at no more than about $+0.9$ F1 over counting here. The pattern lookup is not one scheme among many; by Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} it bounds the whole class. The bound is on the population optimum, and the estimated lookup can be beaten in sample where cells are thin; Section~\ref{sec:rq3} reports the one benchmark where that happens.

\paragraph{Sensitivity at the achieved sample size.}
We report sensitivity at the achieved sample size rather than observed power, which is a
deterministic function of the $p$-value and uninformative. With 777 paired questions and the
bootstrap the intervals use, the smallest effect this design detects at $80\%$ power is $0.92$
F1, and a two one-sided tests procedure establishes equivalence to zero within $\pm 0.83$ F1 at
$\alpha = 0.05$. Effects above roughly $0.9$ F1 are therefore excluded, while $+0.30$ cannot be
separated from zero at this $n$. The same design resolves the method's $+2.50$ comfortably,
against a minimum detectable effect of $1.13$.
% [claim:C051] [evidence:E127]

The sweep agrees: correlation and diversity weighting selected $\lambda = 0$ in all five folds, an explicit pairwise co-assertion parameterisation with 28 indicators gained $+0.05$, and question-domain weights gained $+0.00$. % [claim:C025] [evidence:E111]

The support-count profile shows why counting already does most of the filtering here: $P(\mathrm{correct} \mid \text{exactly } k \text{ agents assert})$ runs $1.2$, $11.1$, $17.4$, $27.2$, $36.5$, $46.3$, $51.9$ and $56.5$ percent for $k = 1 \dots 8$. % [claim:C026] [evidence:E122]
A solo claim is rarely correct and two agreeing agents raise the odds by roughly an order of magnitude, so a support-count threshold is close to the best this class of information supports.

\subsection{RQ2: what lies outside the bound}
\label{sec:rq2}

The lower two rows of Table~\ref{tab:main} answer RQ2. The confidence slot moves $29.21$ to $30.68$ with a global threshold, and the decision slot adds $1.02$ more to reach $31.70$; both intervals exclude zero, and the two axes are close to independent. In the ablation sweep the confidence signals alone are worth $+1.44$ over counting, and the budget alone --- \texttt{adapt-count}, which uses no agent identity at all --- is worth $+1.19$. % [claim:C027] [evidence:E111]
Agent identity is worth roughly $+0.3$ on top of either. The literature this work extends places its weight on agent identity; here the two signals that ignore it are worth about five times the one that does not.

Within the confidence slot, omission strength is the largest single block: alone it is worth $+1.65$ over counting.
% [claim:C028] [evidence:E111]

\paragraph{Ablations, in distribution.}
Gradient boosting over the same features scores $29.95$ alone and $31.73$ with the budget; a logistic/boosting blend scores $30.80$ and $32.06$. % [claim:C029] [evidence:E100]
The blended arm is the highest in-distribution number we measured. It is not our reported result, for the transfer reason in Section~\ref{sec:robust}.

\paragraph{Off-the-shelf fusion.}
Of the classical rank-fusion rules only Reciprocal Rank Fusion beats counting, at $+0.84$; Borda, CombSUM and weighted Borda land between $-0.42$ and $-0.67$. % [claim:C030] [evidence:E111]

\subsection{Breadth: every ensemble, not one pool}
\label{sec:breadth}

To test whether the 8-agent pool was favourable, we evaluate all 210 subsets of sizes 3 to 6 (Appendix~\ref{app:ensembles}). Our method beats counting in all 210 with intervals excluding zero, by $+4.43$, $+4.53$, $+3.13$ and $+2.31$ F1 at sizes 3 to 6; reliability weighting manages 105 of 210 and turns negative at size 6.


% [claim:C031] [evidence:E102]


Appendix~\ref{app:figs} plots all 210 QAMPARI and 42 ASQA ensembles individually, showing the spread behind these medians and the reversal between benchmarks.

The size-6 row locates the crossover in support-pattern rules specifically, not in aggregation itself: weighting and the pattern lookup both decay toward zero as the pool grows and weighting turns negative, while the output-structure arms cross over nowhere we can measure.
% [claim:C032] [evidence:E102]

\subsection{Robustness}
\label{sec:robust}

\paragraph{Holding out a whole question type.}
Random folds draw train and test from one distribution, so a rule exploiting something peculiar to QAMPARI as a whole would still look fine. We therefore train on four of its five question sources and test on the fifth, five times; we call this leave-one-source-out, or LOSO (Table~\ref{tab:loso}).

\begin{table}
  \caption{Leave-one-source-out against random 5-fold cross-fitting on QAMPARI. Under a complete change of question type our method loses $0.21$ F1 against a gain of $2.50$, so roughly $92\%$ survives and stays significant against counting. The boosted blend is the best arm under random folds and the worst transfer of any, which is why it is not part of the method.}
  \label{tab:loso}
  \centering
  \begin{tabular}{lrrrl}
    \toprule
    Arm & Random 5-fold & LOSO & Shift & LOSO vs.\ count \\
    \midrule
    count                        & 29.21 & 29.21 & $+0.00$ & (reference) \\
    pattern lookup               & 29.50 & 29.44 & $-0.06$ & $+0.24\ [-0.38,+0.80]$ \\
    ours: scorer                 & 30.68 & 30.57 & $-0.11$ & $+1.36\ [+0.66,+2.05]^*$ \\
    \textbf{ours: scorer + budget} & \textbf{31.70} & \textbf{31.49} & $\mathbf{-0.21}$ & $\mathbf{+2.29\ [+1.47,+3.10]^*}$ \\
    logistic/boosting blend + budget & 32.29 & 31.43 & $-0.86$ & $+2.22\ [+1.40,+3.05]^*$ \\
    \bottomrule
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

% [claim:C033] [evidence:E101]

The extra capacity does not pay out of distribution: the blend gains $+0.36$ over the plain logistic under random folds and loses $0.06$ under shift. We take the out-of-distribution ordering as decision-relevant, drop boosting, and report the boosted arms as an ablation.

Gains are positive on every held-out source. For the blended arm they run $+1.41$ (\texttt{wikidata\_simple}, $n=327$), $+2.26$ (\texttt{wikidata\_intersection}, $n=164$), $+2.80$ (\texttt{wikidata\_comp}, $n=149$), $+3.31$ (\texttt{wikitables\_composition}, $n=84$) and $+3.73$ (\texttt{wikitables\_simple}, $n=53$). % [claim:C034] [evidence:E101]
The log reports this breakdown for the blended arm rather than for the method as reported.

\paragraph{A claim we retract.}
Earlier versions argued the pattern lookup collapses under this shift, $+0.25$ to $+0.02$, reading that as the protocol detecting memorisation our features survive. It does not survive the parsing fix: post-fix the arm moves $+0.30$ to $+0.24$, and its interval included zero under either protocol, so the collapse was movement between two non-significant numbers. The protocol discriminates, separating the boosted arm from the plain one, but does not indict the lookup. % [claim:C035] [evidence:E101]

\paragraph{How much supervision this needs.}
The gain saturates by roughly 200 labelled questions and the draw-to-draw spread collapses from $\pm 1.64$ to $\pm 0.15$ F1; at 25 questions the method loses to counting (Appendix~\ref{app:figs}).
% [claim:C036] [evidence:E108]
Open-ended questions are all different, so it is not obvious that coefficients fitted on one set transfer. The features are normalised by their own question's quantities, so they describe how these models emit lists rather than any question's subject matter, and by $n \approx 200$ which training questions were used stops mattering.

\subsection{RQ3: predicting the regime, and the benchmark where we lose}
\label{sec:rq3}


% [claim:C037] [evidence:E118]

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig4_regimes.pdf}
  \caption{Multi-agent counting against the best single agent in the same ensemble, for all 210 QAMPARI subsets and all 42 ASQA subsets of sizes 3 to 6. Faint points are individual ensembles, the line is the mean at each size, and the dashed line is the best single agent. The two benchmarks run in opposite directions: on QAMPARI counting closes a $2.55$ deficit and passes the best agent at six agents, while on ASQA it falls further behind, from $-0.70$ to $-2.26$. The solo ratio of Section~\ref{sec:screen} predicts which regime a benchmark is in before any method is built: $0.06$ on QAMPARI, $0.24$ on TriviaQA, $0.47$ on ASQA. QAMPARI numbers predate the parsing fix of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}.}
  \label{fig:regimes}
\end{figure}

The two per-ensemble sweeps are mirror images: on QAMPARI our method beats reliability weighting in all 210 ensembles, and on ASQA the ordering reverses (Table~\ref{tab:asqa}).


% [claim:C038] [evidence:E104]


\paragraph{On ASQA, aggregating by agreement barely works at all.}
Every arm here should be read against the best single agent rather than against reliability weighting. On the full six-agent pool that agent scores $27.03$; counting scores $24.77$, $2.27$ below, interval excluding zero; weighting recovers to $27.45$, $+0.42$ over it; our scorer lands at $26.81$, $-0.22$, interval including zero. Across the sweep counting falls below the best agent in 39 of 42 ensembles, never significantly beats it, and the deficit grows from $-0.70$ at $n=3$ to $-2.26$ at $n=6$.
% [claim:C048] [evidence:E125,E126]
The whole family of claim-level filters, ours included, is competing to match one agent rather than improve on an ensemble.

\paragraph{The solo ratio said so in advance.}
On ASQA a solo claim is $47\%$ as likely to be correct as a supported one; on QAMPARI, $6\%$. Counting deletes solo claims, so on ASQA roughly half of what it deletes is correct, and it deletes more as the pool grows and more unshared content becomes available --- the mechanism behind the widening deficit. Weighting recovers because per-agent weights let a strong agent's unsupported claims survive a cut counting applies uniformly.

\paragraph{Our confidence slot is empty, so the scorer adds variance rather than signal.}
The QAMPARI position signal is conditional on list length. Table~\ref{tab:rank} restricts to solo assertions, removing the support confound, and stratifies by the asserter's own list length, removing the verbosity confound.


% [claim:C039] [evidence:E105]

ASQA responses average $3.5$ sentences and $79\%$ are 3 or 4, inside the band where QAMPARI shows no signal. We predicted this before measuring: the Spearman correlation between sentence position and correctness is $-0.029$ in that band ($n=4{,}064$) and $-0.047$ pooled over solo claims. % [claim:C040] [evidence:E106]
The consequence shows in where the scorer lands relative to the ceiling it builds on. It takes the pattern probability as a feature and adds the confidence signal, so it should sit above the lookup where that signal informs and below by the extra estimation cost where it does not. On QAMPARI it sits $1.18$ above; on ASQA, $0.39$ below, interval including zero.
% [claim:C049] [evidence:E125,E100]
The model pays for the parameters and gets nothing back: every ASQA response-length band falls inside the range where QAMPARI shows no usable signal (Figure~\ref{fig:position}).

\paragraph{The decision slot is not the problem.}
Metric-matched budget variants for ASQA score $24.98$ to $26.70$, all below the swept threshold, so the failure is upstream in stage 3, not stage 5. % [claim:C041] [evidence:E103]
The two slots can therefore fail independently, and here exactly one of them is at fault.

\paragraph{One reading that cuts against us.}
On ASQA reliability weighting scores $27.45$ against the pattern lookup's $27.20$, so a constrained rule beats the Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} bound by $+0.25$, interval excluding zero. With 6,015 clusters over $2^6$ cells the lookup estimates on far thinner data than QAMPARI's 110,136 claims over $2^8$, the finite-sample case Section~\ref{sec:ceiling} sets aside. This limits the empirical bound rather than the construction, and is a second reading of the same data shortage that leaves the confidence slot empty. % [claim:C042] [evidence:E103]

\paragraph{Scope of this result.}
The template's precondition is real, checkable before any implementation, and a benchmark can sit where the expensive slot is not worth filling. Sentence position is not useless in general; it is flat at ASQA's response lengths. We checked the obvious alternative explanation: the ASQA scorer lacks the stacked pattern probability the QAMPARI scorer uses, so it might have been handicapped on the channel that matters most here. Adding it is worth $+0.01$ DR* over the 42 ensembles and the scorer still sits below the lookup, so the gap is not an artefact of that omission.
% [claim:C050] [evidence:E128]

\paragraph{Scope of the screen.}
The solo-ratio ordering is consistent across our three benchmarks and mechanistically connected to what counting can do, but we have not validated it on a benchmark held out from its construction, and it supports an ordering only.

\subsection{Where the method is loose}
\label{sec:calib}


% [claim:C043] [evidence:E110]

Because the budget substitutes $\sum p_i$ for the unknown correct count, the overconfidence in Table~\ref{tab:calib} overstates expected hits and biases $k$ toward keeping too much. This explains why isotonic calibration changed nothing: a globally fitted curve is dominated by the bottom bin, already accurate, and barely moves the 3,115 claims above $0.35$ the decision depends on. A high-$p$-weighted recalibration is untried. It cannot threaten the scorer's own result, since the ordering is calibration-invariant and the $30.68$ row of Table~\ref{tab:main} stands regardless; only the budget's $+1.02$ depends on the sums.

\subsection{Negative results}
\label{sec:negative}

The full sweep is in Appendix~\ref{app:negative}; each entry rules out an approach that would otherwise look untried, cross-fitted against counting on QAMPARI. Three have mechanisms worth giving here.


% [claim:C044] [evidence:E111]

String-similar QAMPARI entities are usually genuinely different --- sequels, or people sharing a surname --- rather than aliases, and a false merge deletes a correct answer outright since only the cluster representative survives. At a $0.70$ threshold, 14 good merges came with 413 bad. % [claim:C045] [evidence:E111]
The same asymmetry sinks an LLM grouping call in place of edit-distance clustering: it correctly merges \texttt{Warner Bros} with \texttt{Warner Bros. Pictures}, but also \texttt{UMass Amherst} with \texttt{University of Nebraska}, at a $7.6\%$ merge rate.
% [claim:C046] [evidence:E123]

One number looks like available headroom. Keeping the best prefix per question, on the ordering the scorer already produces, scores $39.90$ --- the maximum of roughly 143 noisy prefix scores per question, heavily selection-biased. Every attempt to collect it fell far short: a learned $k$ scored $25.64$, a constant $k$ $29.41$, and three changes to the budget objective each moved the result by at most $0.18$. % [claim:C047] [evidence:E111]
It is an artefact of post-hoc selection.


\section{Related work}
\label{sec:related}

Appendix~\ref{app:related} treats each of these lines in full.

\paragraph{Aggregating outputs across models.}
Majority voting over sampled responses \cite{wang2022self}, scaled with agent count \cite{li2024agents}, with weights from higher-order agreement information \cite{ai2025majority}; a parallel branch selects or fuses whole responses \cite{jiang2023llm,chen2023universal}, and theory bounds what any policy returning one member's answer can achieve \cite{ananthakrishnan2026power}. The estimand throughout is a discrete label or a whole response; ours is a claim, and our aggregator emits a set assembled from fragments of several responses, so it is not bounded by any member's answer.

\paragraph{Decomposing long-form answers into claims.}
Self-consistency generalises to clustering atomic units and keeping clusters by member count \cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic}, and atomic decomposition is now infrastructure for factuality evaluation and calibration \cite{zhang2024atomic,ning2025mad,wan2025fastfact,liu2025verifact,goel2025zero,panou2025harnessing}. All draw samples from one model, or use several only to check claims, so agent identity is not a variable. We make it one, and find it worth less than the non-identity signals beside it.

\paragraph{Item-level reliability without labels.}
Annotator-reliability models from crowdsourcing \cite{dawid1979maximum,sinha2018fast,majdi2023crowd,kuzin2025improving}, and label-free behavioural signals read from the model itself: response length \cite{janiak2025illusion,zhang2024verbosity}, consistency under perturbation \cite{wu2026this,ahn2025prompt}. Position effects are well documented, but on the input side \cite{guo2024serial,zhang2026position,salvatore2025lost,qiao2026llm,yan2026evirank}; conformal prediction sizes sets for coverage rather than F1 \cite{gauthier2025adaptive}.

\paragraph{How ours differs, on three axes.}
On granularity, that work scores whole responses while we score each claim. On position, every result above concerns a sequence given to the model, whereas our signal is an item's position in the model's own free-form output --- a different phenomenon, and we read the nearest work in full to verify the distinction \cite{guo2024serial}. On evidence source, the reliability literature models which agent said what; we also grade silence by how much the silent agent said, which we know of no prior work doing. One reported result conflicts with our position measurement; Appendix~\ref{app:related} reconciles them \cite{islam2025curious}.


\section{Conclusion and discussion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Aggregation methods have grown more sophisticated about one observation: which agents asserted a claim. We separated it from a second, what each agent's generation reveals about that claim, and measured both. On QAMPARI the strongest claim-local identity-only rule we can estimate beats counting by $+0.30$ set F1, with an interval placing it no higher than about $+0.9$. What remains lies in how each agent presented its answer: where a claim sits in that agent's output, and how much the agents omitting it produced instead. A scorer over those signals with a metric-matched keep-count reaches $+4.30$ over the best single agent and $+2.50$ over counting, holds in all 210 agent subsets, and keeps roughly $92\%$ of its gain on question types never seen in training.

The template has two slots, each keyed to a different property of the task: the answer format determines what confidence signal exists, the metric what decision rule is admissible. Both bound where it applies: the confidence slot as instantiated requires list-structured output and the keep-count a metric with a precision term, so neither is defined for single-answer tasks; on ASQA the slot is empty and we lose to reliability weighting, both outcome and mechanism predicted in advance by a ratio computable from the generations and by a measurement showing the position signal is absent at ASQA response lengths.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
\label{sec:limitations}
The position signal has a measured domain of validity: it is flat or mildly reversed on lists of five items or fewer, and the QAMPARI gain depends on $70\%$ of solo assertions sitting in lists of 101 or more items. The screen rests on three benchmarks, none held out from its construction, and supports an ordering only (Section~\ref{sec:rq3}). Every arm is supervised, ours most heavily, with a floor near 50 labelled questions and saturation near 200 (Section~\ref{sec:robust}). The keep-count is fed by probabilities that are overconfident in exactly the region it reads, and a high-$p$ recalibration is untried (Section~\ref{sec:calib}). Our ASC baseline substitutes agents for stochastic samples, changing the estimand to inter-model agreement and leaving the published single-model form unevaluated (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}). $\mathrm{DR}^*$ substitutes STR-EM for Disambig-F1, and a faithful DR requires a RoBERTa-SQuADv2 reader (Section~\ref{sec:background}). QAMPARI grading is close to exact string match, costing roughly 7 F1 near-uniformly across arms (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}). All agents are proprietary API models sampled once at a single decoding setting; we do not measure sensitivity to temperature or to repeated sampling.

\paragraph{Three result tables predate a parsing fix.}
Tables~\ref{tab:ens-gain}, \ref{tab:ens-sig} and \ref{tab:negative} were computed before the normalisation fix of Section~\ref{sec:protocol} and are pending regeneration. The fix moved every affected QAMPARI arm by at most $0.14$ F1 and altered no significance verdict where it has been rerun, but for these tables that is an expectation rather than a measurement.

An agent that generates tells you more than which claims it endorsed, and on this task most of the usable signal was in the part we had been discarding.


