\appendix

\section{Technical appendices and supplementary material}

\subsection{What the decomposition implies}
\label{sec:theory}

The first two statements below are elementary; they fix which quantity the experiments have to measure. The third derives the decision rule of Section~\ref{sec:slotB}.

\paragraph{Proposition 1 (information dominance).}
\emph{Let $R^*(\mathcal{O})$ denote the Bayes risk of the best decision rule measurable with respect to an observation $\mathcal{O}$, under any fixed loss. Then $R^*(S,B) \leq R^*(S)$, with equality whenever $Y_c \perp B(c) \mid S(c)$.}

Any rule using $S$ alone is also a rule using $(S,B)$ that ignores its second argument, so the infimum over the larger class cannot be higher. Behavioural observations can only help if they carry information about correctness that the identity of the asserting agents does not already carry, which is a property of the data rather than of the method. That is the quantity Section~\ref{sec:rq2} measures.

Both consequences occur in our experiments. This is a statement about the population optimum, not about a fitted model: with finite data, adding features that carry no signal costs estimation variance, so a richer observation can and does lose. Section~\ref{sec:rq3} reports exactly this on ASQA, where our scorer lands below the identity-only rule it builds on. The proposition also says nothing about how much $B$ helps, only that the sign cannot be negative in the population, so nothing empirical follows from it alone.

\paragraph{Proposition 2 (behaviour as likelihood evidence).}
\emph{Write $X_{ic}$ for agent $i$'s complete observation about claim $c$, which is either an assertion together with its position, or silence together with the amount that agent produced. If the $X_{ic}$ are conditionally independent given $Y_c$, then}
\[
  \mathrm{logit} P(Y_c = 1 \mid X_{1c}, \dots, X_{nc})
  \;=\; \mathrm{logit} \pi \;+\; \sum_{i=1}^{n} \log \frac{P(X_{ic} \mid Y_c = 1)}{P(X_{ic} \mid Y_c = 0)} ,
\]
\emph{where $\pi$ is the prior probability that a pooled candidate is correct.}

This is Bayes' rule under the stated independence assumption, and it locates reliability weighting precisely. Collapse $X_{ic}$ to the assertion indicator $\mathbf{1}[i \in S(c)]$ and each agent contributes $\log(\mathrm{TPR}_i / \mathrm{FPR}_i)$ when it asserts and $\log((1-\mathrm{TPR}_i)/(1-\mathrm{FPR}_i))$ when it is silent. The posterior log-odds is then an additive score over agents, which is a weighted vote; assuming further that each agent is characterised by a single accuracy $x_i$ with symmetric errors recovers the log-odds weight $\log(x_i / (1 - x_i))$ of the optimal-weight construction \cite{ai2025majority}. Reliability weighting is therefore not a competing family but the special case of this decomposition obtained by discarding position and stopping information.

The independence assumption does not hold here: agents share training data and see the same question, and our own measurements show their errors are correlated. The proposition still identifies the functional form the evidence should take, namely additive per-agent contributions on the log-odds scale, which is what our scorer fits, and it explains why we fit that form discriminatively rather than estimating the likelihoods: a logistic regression over the same observations recovers the additive structure without committing to a generative model, and its coefficients absorb dependence that a Naive Bayes estimator would misattribute. The empirical sweep is consistent with this choice, since an explicit correlation-aware weighting selected $\lambda = 0$ in all five folds and a pairwise co-assertion parameterisation gained $+0.05$.

\paragraph{Proposition 3 (the metric-matched decision).}
\emph{For a question with $G$ gold answers, keeping $k$ candidates of which $H$ are correct gives}
\[
  F_1 \;=\; \frac{2PR}{P+R} \;=\; \frac{2H}{k+G},
\]
\emph{since $P = H/k$ and $R = H/G$. If the scorer emits calibrated probabilities and candidates are taken in descending order, then $\mathbb{E}[H] = \sum_{j \leq k} p_{(j)}$, and for known $G$ the expected F1 is exactly $2\sum_{j\leq k} p_{(j)} / (k+G)$.}

The denominator is constant in $H$, so taking the expectation through the ratio is exact once $G$ is fixed; the only approximation in the rule of Section~\ref{sec:slotB} is substituting $\hat{G}_q$ for $G$. Writing $C(k) = \sum_{j \leq k} p_{(j)}$, the objective increases from $k$ to $k+1$ exactly when
\[
  p_{(k+1)} \;>\; \frac{C(k)}{k + \hat{G}_q} \;=\; \frac{1}{2}\widehat{F_1}(k),
\]
so the rule has a simple reading: keep taking candidates while the next probability exceeds half the expected F1 achieved so far, and stop otherwise. This makes the sensitivity to $\hat{G}_q$ visible. The estimate enters only through a threshold that moves slowly in $\hat{G}_q$, which is why a mean absolute error of $4.62$ answers against a gold mean of $10.2$ costs so little, and why substituting the true count gains only $+0.55$.

\paragraph{What the propositions leave to measurement.}
Together they say that behavioural observations cannot hurt in the population, that reliability weighting is the special case that ignores them, and that a calibrated scorer implies its own stopping rule. None of them says whether real language models actually place correct claims differently from incorrect ones, or stop generating in an informative way. That is what Sections~\ref{sec:rq1} to~\ref{sec:rq3} are for, and on one of our three benchmarks the answer is no.


\subsection{Extended related work}
\label{app:related}

\paragraph{Aggregating outputs across models.}
The line runs from majority voting over sampled responses \cite{wang2022self} through scaling it with agent count \cite{li2024agents} to deriving weights from higher-order agreement information \cite{ai2025majority}; a parallel branch selects or fuses whole responses, by pairwise ranking with generative fusion \cite{jiang2023llm} or model-based selection of the most consistent one \cite{chen2023universal}, and theory bounds what any policy whose output is one member's answer can achieve \cite{ananthakrishnan2026power}. Its estimand is a discrete label or a whole response; ours is a claim, and our aggregator emits a set assembled from fragments of several responses, so it is not bounded by any member's answer.

\paragraph{Decomposing long-form answers into claims.}
Self-consistency over sampled reasoning paths \cite{wang2022self} generalises to selecting the most consistent response \cite{chen2023universal}, then to clustering atomic units and keeping clusters by member count \cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic}. Atomic decomposition is now infrastructure for factuality evaluation and calibration \cite{zhang2024atomic}, including multi-agent evaluators \cite{ning2025mad,wan2025fastfact,liu2025verifact} and cross-model consistency checking at claim granularity \cite{goel2025zero}; a plain union of list answers has been used for biomedical QA \cite{panou2025harnessing}. All draw samples from one model, or use several only to check claims, so agent identity does not exist as a variable. We make it available and find it worth less than the non-identity signals beside it. ASC's own paper concedes model-based decomposition would give better atomic units than its sentence splitter but was too expensive; our ASQA pipeline shares that limitation.

\paragraph{Item-level reliability without labels.}
Annotator-reliability models originate in crowdsourcing \cite{dawid1979maximum,sinha2018fast,majdi2023crowd} and carry to deep ensembles \cite{kuzin2025improving}. A separate strand reads label-free behavioural signals from the model itself: response length as a hallucination detector \cite{janiak2025illusion,zhang2024verbosity}, consistency under prompt perturbation as a credibility measure \cite{wu2026this,ahn2025prompt}. Position effects are well documented, but on the input side --- which supplied label a model selects \cite{guo2024serial}, locating items in a given list \cite{zhang2026position}, context position \cite{salvatore2025lost}, positional bias when reranking candidates \cite{qiao2026llm,yan2026evirank}. Adaptive set sizing exists in conformal prediction, targeting coverage rather than F1 \cite{gauthier2025adaptive}.

\paragraph{How ours differs, on three axes.}
On granularity, that work scores whole responses while we score each claim. On position, every result above concerns a sequence given to the model, whereas our signal is an item's position in the model's own free-form output --- a different phenomenon, and we read the nearest work in full to verify the distinction \cite{guo2024serial}. On evidence source, the reliability literature models which agent said what, while we also grade silence by how much the silent agent said, which we know of no prior work doing. One finding does concern generated position: accuracy falling from $51.3\%$ for the first requested fact to $30.1\%$ for the fifth across 16 models \cite{islam2025curious}, in tension with Table~\ref{tab:rank}, which shows no gradient across the first five. Their setting requests a fixed number of facts, so later positions are produced under pressure, while our short lists are self-truncated because the model had no more to give; in our long lists, under enumerative pressure, the gradient reappears. We offer this as a hypothesis consistent with both measurements.

